{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"gaming-pc-build-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Gaming PC build track","description":"Takes a first-time builder from goals and budget to a parts list, a compatibility check, a guided assembly and first boot with drivers and tuning, with approval between steps.","category":"video-games","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","verify"],"role":["gamer","individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences"],"output":["plan","table","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["pc-building","gaming-pc","parts-list","hardware-compatibility","first-boot"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["choose-computer-specs","speed-up-slow-computer"]},"args":[{"name":"goals","description":"The games you want to play, at what resolution and frame rate, and anything else the PC must do (streaming, editing).","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"budget","description":"Total budget and currency, and whether it includes monitor, peripherals and operating system.","type":"string","required":true},{"name":"reuse","description":"Parts you already own and want to reuse (monitor, storage, case, power supply). Optional.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"requirements","file":"steps/01-requirements.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"parts-list","file":"steps/02-parts-list.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"compatibility","file":"steps/03-compatibility.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve"},{"id":"assembly","file":"steps/04-assembly.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve"},{"id":"first-boot","file":"steps/05-first-boot.md","stage":"verify","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Guides a first gaming PC build the way an experienced builder helps a friend: decide what the PC is for, spend where it shows in games, check every part fits and works together, assemble carefully, and finish with a stable, tuned system. Each step stops for approval.\n\n<build>\nGoals: {{goals}}\nBudget: {{budget}}\n{{#reuse}}Reusing: {{reuse}}{{/reuse}}\n</build>\n\nRules for every step: never state current prices, stock or exact model specifications as fact; give budget shares and tiers, and tell the builder to confirm specifications on the manufacturer's page and prices in their country. Mark every compatibility claim you are not certain of as \"check\". Do not suggest unofficial software, cracked operating systems or driver-updater tools. Do one step at a time and wait for approval.","steps":[{"id":"requirements","file":"steps/01-requirements.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Requirements and budget split\n\n1. Restate the goals as a performance target: resolution, target frame rate, the most demanding game on the list, and any non-gaming workloads.\n2. Ask at most five questions that change the build: country, what the budget covers, size and noise preferences, upgrade plans, Wi-Fi need, and comfort with building.\n3. Explain which part sets the target: for most gaming at higher resolutions the graphics card, for high-refresh competitive play or simulation-heavy games also the processor.\n4. Propose a budget split as percentages (graphics card, processor, motherboard, memory, storage, power supply, case and cooling, operating system), adjusted for reused parts.\n5. Say honestly if the target does not fit the budget and offer the trade-off (lower target, used parts, saving longer).\n\nStop for approval of the target and split."},{"id":"parts-list","file":"steps/02-parts-list.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Parts list\n\n1. For each component, describe the tier and the specifications to look for, with one or two example product families you are confident exist, marked to verify against current listings.\n2. Cover: processor and socket, motherboard chipset and size, memory amount and type, storage type and capacity, graphics card tier and video memory, power supply wattage with headroom and an efficiency rating from a reputable maker, case airflow and size, cooler, and the operating system licence from an official source.\n3. Note where to save safely (case, storage capacity) and where not to (power supply quality).\n4. Give the list as a table the builder fills in with the exact models and local prices they pick.\n\nStop for approval. Ask the builder to paste the exact models they chose."},{"id":"compatibility","file":"steps/03-compatibility.md","stage":"verify","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Compatibility check\n\nCheck the builder's exact models:\n1. Processor socket matches the motherboard; the motherboard may need a firmware update for this processor (check the maker's support list).\n2. Memory type matches the board; two sticks in the right slots for dual channel.\n3. Graphics card length and thickness fit the case; cooler height fits the case.\n4. Power supply wattage covers the maker's recommendation for the card, with the right connectors.\n5. Storage connections and slots are available.\n6. Motherboard size fits the case.\n\nPresent a table: Check | Result (pass, fail or check) | How to confirm. Flag any fail with a swap.\n\nStop for approval before ordering or building."},{"id":"assembly","file":"steps/04-assembly.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Assembly\n\n1. Prepare: clear table off carpet, good light, a Phillips screwdriver, the motherboard and case manuals open. Discharge static by touching bare metal on the case or a grounded object, handle boards by their edges, and keep parts in their anti-static bags until used.\n2. Electrical safety: keep the power supply switched off and unplugged from the wall whenever your hands are inside the case. Never open the power supply itself; it holds a dangerous charge even when unplugged. If it has a voltage selector, set it for your country's mains before first use.\n3. Build order on the box before the case: processor, cooler with thermal paste if not pre-applied, memory in the slots the manual names, the main storage drive.\n4. Into the case: standoffs matching the board, the I/O shield if separate, board, power supply, graphics card, front-panel connectors by the manual, fans for front intake and rear or top exhaust.\n5. Cable checks: main power, processor power and graphics power fully seated with no gap or bend at the plug; use the power supply's own cables (never mix modular cables from another unit) and avoid adapters for graphics power unless they came with the card.\n6. Common mistakes to avoid: missing standoffs, memory not clicked in, display cable plugged into the motherboard instead of the graphics card, forgotten processor power cable.\n\nStop for approval. Ask the builder to confirm each cable check before powering on."},{"id":"first-boot","file":"steps/05-first-boot.md","stage":"verify","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: First boot, drivers and tuning\n\n1. First power on: with the side panel off, nothing loose in the case and fans clear of cables, plug in and switch on. If you see sparks, smell burning or hear a pop, switch off at the wall at once and do not retry until the cause is found. Then enter the firmware setup, confirm the processor, memory amount and drives are detected, and check temperatures at idle.\n2. If it does not boot: read the board's debug lights or beeps per the manual, reseat memory, check power cables and the display cable.\n3. Install the operating system from official media, then chipset and graphics drivers from the manufacturers' sites.\n4. Enable the memory's rated speed profile in firmware, then test stability with a demanding game for an hour while watching temperatures.\n5. Set the monitor's refresh rate in the operating system, then tune each game's settings for the target from step 1.\n6. Finish with a checklist: backups, firmware update plan, warranty registration, and keeping boxes for the return window."}]}
